<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Gopallawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Gopallawa was the last Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a>, from 1962, and the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, from 1972 to 1978. A lawyer and civil servant from Matale who had served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, he became the ceremonial head of state when the republican constitution of 1972 ended dominion status. He stepped down when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-r-jayewardene">J. R. Jayewardene</a> took the new executive presidency in 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-gopallawa.jpg" alt="William Gopallawa" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abbie Rowe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JFKWHP-AR6771-B_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
